<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="section-cross-product" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>The Cross Product</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this chapter, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Understand the cross product as an operation that produces a vector
perpendicular to two given vectors, with its direction fixed by the
right-hand rule.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Interpret the magnitude of the cross product as the area of the parallelogram
spanned by the two vectors, and recognize that the cross product is zero
exactly when the vectors are parallel.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Understand why the geometric and component definitions of the cross product
describe one and the same object.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Contrast the cross product with the dot product: one returns a vector and
detects parallelism, while the other returns a scalar and detects
perpendicularity.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Understand torque as a measure of the turning effect of a force, and
appreciate the role of cross product in determining torque.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<section xml:id="sec-cross-definition">
<title>Definition and Properties of the Cross Product</title>

<definition xml:id="def-cross-product">
<title>The Cross Product (First Definition)</title>
<statement>
<p>
Given two vectors <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m> in
<m>\mathbb R^3</m>, the <term>cross product</term>
<m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf v</m> is the <em>vector</em> defined by
<md xml:id="eq-cross-geometric" number="yes">
\mathbf u \times \mathbf v = \left(|\mathbf u||\mathbf v|\sin\theta\right)\mathbf n,
</md>
where <m>\theta</m>, with <m>0 \le \theta \le \pi</m>, is the angle
between <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m>, and <m>\mathbf n</m> is
the unit vector orthogonal to both <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m>
whose direction is given by the right-hand rule.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
The magnitude of <m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf v</m> is equal to the area
of the parallelogram built on the two vectors <m>\mathbf u</m> and
<m>\mathbf v</m>, as shown in <xref ref="fig-parallelogram-area"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-parallelogram-area">
<caption>
The parallelogram built on the vectors <m>\mathbf u</m> and
<m>\mathbf v</m>, with base <m>|\mathbf v|</m> and height
<m>|\mathbf u||\sin(\theta)|</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-parallelogram-area">
<diagram dimensions="(340,200)" margins="10">
<definition>u=(2,3)</definition>
<definition>v=(5,0)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-0.6,7.5,3.6)">
<line at="par-side-top" endpoints="(u, u+v)" stroke="red" thickness="2"/>
<line at="par-side-right" endpoints="(v, u+v)" stroke="blue" thickness="2"/>
<line at="par-height" endpoints="(u, (2,0))" stroke="black" dash="4 4"/>
<vector at="par-vec-u" v="u" tail="(0,0)" stroke="blue"/>
<vector at="par-vec-v" v="v" tail="(0,0)" stroke="red"/>
<label at="par-u-label" anchor="(1,1.5)" alignment="nw" color="blue">
<m>\mathbf u</m>
</label>
<label at="par-v-label" anchor="(2.3,-0.2)" alignment="s" color="red">
<m>\mathbf v</m>
</label>
<label at="par-height-label" anchor="(2.1,1.5)" alignment="e">
<m>|\mathbf u|\sin(\theta)</m>
</label>
<angle-marker at="par-angle-theta" points="(v,(0,0),u)" radius="20" clear-background="no">
<m>\theta</m>
</angle-marker>
</coordinates>
<annotations text="A parallelogram built on the vectors u and v, drawn tail to tail from the origin, with v along the horizontal direction. A dashed vertical segment from the tip of u down to v marks the height of the parallelogram, equal to the length of u times the sine of theta.">
<annotation ref="par-vec-u" text="Vector u, drawn from the origin at angle theta above v."/>
<annotation ref="par-vec-v" text="Vector v, along the horizontal direction."/>
<annotation ref="par-side-top" text="The side of the parallelogram parallel to v, starting at the tip of u."/>
<annotation ref="par-side-right" text="The side of the parallelogram parallel to u, starting at the tip of v."/>
<annotation ref="par-height" text="The dashed height of the parallelogram, with length equal to the length of u times the sine of theta."/>
<annotation ref="par-angle-theta" text="The angle theta between u and v, located at the origin."/>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<fact xml:id="fact-parallelogram-area">
<title>Area of the Parallelogram</title>
<statement>
<p>
<md xml:id="eq-parallelogram-area" number="yes">
\text{Area of the parallelogram}
= |\mathbf u||\mathbf v||\sin\theta|
= |\mathbf u \times \mathbf v|.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</fact>

<p>
Note that <m>|\mathbf u||\mathbf v||\sin\theta|</m> is the length of the
cross product <m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf v</m>. Since
<m>\sin(\theta)</m> can be negative, we need to include its absolute
value.
</p>

<p>
The vector <m>\mathbf n</m> is the unit vector that determines the
direction of <m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf v</m> using the right-hand
rule. Note that <m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf v</m> is orthogonal
(perpendicular) to both vectors, as shown in
<xref ref="fig-cross-product-video"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cross-product-video">
<caption>
The cross product <m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf v</m> points in the
direction of the unit vector <m>\mathbf n</m>, given by the right-hand
rule, and is orthogonal to both <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m>;
its length is <m>|\mathbf u||\mathbf v||\sin\theta|</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-cross-product"
       source="videos/cross_product_3d_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-right-hand-rule-illustration">
  <caption>Two equivalent right-hand rules for <m>\mathbf{u}\times\mathbf{v}</m>.</caption>
  <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="2%">
    <figure xml:id="fig-rhr-imt">
      <caption>Index–Middle–Thumb method.</caption>
      <image source="images/rhr-index-middle-thumb.png" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>Index finger along u, middle finger along v, thumb along u cross v.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <figure xml:id="fig-rhr-curl">
      <caption>Rotating-fingers method.</caption>
      <image source="images/rhr-rotating-fingers.png" width="100%">
        <shortdescription>Fingers curl from u toward v, thumb points along u cross v.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </sidebyside>
</figure>

<fact xml:id="fact-parallel-vectors">
<title>Parallel Vectors</title>
<statement>
<p>
From <xref ref="eq-cross-geometric"/>, it follows that the cross product
of two parallel vectors is zero.
</p>
<p>
<md xml:id="eq-parallel-vectors" number="yes">
\mathbf u \parallel \mathbf v \iff \mathbf u \times \mathbf v = \mathbf 0,
\qquad \mathbf u, \mathbf v \neq \mathbf 0.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</fact>

<theorem xml:id="thm-cross-properties">
<title>Properties of the Cross Product</title>
<statement>
<p>
If <m>\mathbf u</m>, <m>\mathbf v</m>, and <m>\mathbf w</m> are any
vectors and <m>r</m>, <m>s</m> are scalars, then
</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li><p><m>(r\mathbf u) \times (s\mathbf v) = (rs)(\mathbf u \times \mathbf v)</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>\mathbf u \times (\mathbf v + \mathbf w) = \mathbf u \times \mathbf v + \mathbf u \times \mathbf w</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>(\mathbf v + \mathbf w) \times \mathbf u = \mathbf v \times \mathbf u + \mathbf w \times \mathbf u</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>\mathbf v \times \mathbf u = -(\mathbf u \times \mathbf v)</m></p></li>
<li><p><m>\mathbf 0 \times \mathbf u = \mathbf 0</m></p></li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<proof>
<p>
Although the cross product was defined geometrically above, all five
properties are most easily checked using the equivalent component
formula <xref ref="eq-cross-components"/> established in the next
section: if <m>\mathbf a = \langle a_1,a_2,a_3\rangle</m> and
<m>\mathbf b = \langle b_1,b_2,b_3\rangle</m>, then
<m>\mathbf a \times \mathbf b =
\langle a_2b_3-a_3b_2,\; a_3b_1-a_1b_3,\; a_1b_2-a_2b_1\rangle</m>.
Write <m>\mathbf u = \langle u_1,u_2,u_3\rangle</m>,
<m>\mathbf v = \langle v_1,v_2,v_3\rangle</m>, and
<m>\mathbf w = \langle w_1,w_2,w_3\rangle</m>.
</p>
<p>
<alert>(1)</alert> Each component of <m>(r\mathbf u)\times(s\mathbf v)</m>
has the form <m>(ru_i)(sv_j) - (ru_j)(sv_i) = rs(u_iv_j-u_jv_i)</m>,
which is <m>rs</m> times the corresponding component of
<m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf v</m>. Hence
<m>(r\mathbf u)\times(s\mathbf v) = (rs)(\mathbf u \times \mathbf v)</m>.
</p>
<p>
<alert>(2)</alert> The first component of
<m>\mathbf u \times (\mathbf v + \mathbf w)</m> is
<md>
u_2(v_3+w_3) - u_3(v_2+w_2) = (u_2v_3-u_3v_2) + (u_2w_3-u_3w_2),
</md>
which is the sum of the first components of
<m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf v</m> and <m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf w</m>.
The same holds for the other two components, so
<m>\mathbf u \times (\mathbf v+\mathbf w)
= \mathbf u \times \mathbf v + \mathbf u \times \mathbf w</m>.
</p>
<p>
<alert>(3)</alert> By the same computation with the roles of the two
factors exchanged, the first component of
<m>(\mathbf v+\mathbf w)\times \mathbf u</m> is
<m>(v_2+w_2)u_3 - (v_3+w_3)u_2 = (v_2u_3-v_3u_2) + (w_2u_3-w_3u_2)</m>,
the sum of the first components of <m>\mathbf v \times \mathbf u</m> and
<m>\mathbf w \times \mathbf u</m>; likewise for the other two components,
so <m>(\mathbf v+\mathbf w)\times \mathbf u
= \mathbf v \times \mathbf u + \mathbf w \times \mathbf u</m>.
</p>
<p>
<alert>(4)</alert> The first component of <m>\mathbf v \times \mathbf u</m>
is <m>v_2u_3 - v_3u_2 = -(u_2v_3-u_3v_2)</m>, the negative of the first
component of <m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf v</m>; likewise for the other
two components, so
<m>\mathbf v \times \mathbf u = -(\mathbf u \times \mathbf v)</m>.
</p>
<p>
<alert>(5)</alert> Since <m>\mathbf 0 = \langle 0,0,0\rangle</m>, every
component of <m>\mathbf 0 \times \mathbf u</m> is a difference of
products each containing a factor of <m>0</m>, so
<m>\mathbf 0 \times \mathbf u = \mathbf 0</m>.
</p>
</proof>

</section>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<section xml:id="sec-cross-components">
<title>Second Definition of the Cross Product</title>

<p>
Writing the vectors in components gives another definition for the cross
product.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-cross-components">
<title>The Cross Product (Second Definition)</title>
<statement>
<p>
If <m>\mathbf u = \langle u_1, u_2, u_3\rangle</m> and
<m>\mathbf v = \langle v_1, v_2, v_3\rangle</m>, then
<md xml:id="eq-cross-components" number="yes">
\mathbf u \times \mathbf v =
\langle u_2v_3 - u_3v_2,\; u_3v_1 - u_1v_3,\; u_1v_2 - u_2v_1\rangle.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
One way to remember the second definition of the cross product is to
write it as a determinant:
<md xml:id="eq-cross-determinant" number="yes">
\mathbf u \times \mathbf v =
\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf i \amp \mathbf j \amp \mathbf k \\
u_1 \amp u_2 \amp u_3 \\
v_1 \amp v_2 \amp v_3
\end{vmatrix}
=
\begin{vmatrix} u_2 \amp u_3 \\ v_2 \amp v_3 \end{vmatrix}\mathbf i
- \begin{vmatrix} u_1 \amp u_3 \\ v_1 \amp v_3 \end{vmatrix}\mathbf j
+ \begin{vmatrix} u_1 \amp u_2 \\ v_1 \amp v_2 \end{vmatrix}\mathbf k,
</md>
where
<md xml:id="eq-two-by-two-determinant" number="yes">
\begin{vmatrix} a \amp b \\ c \amp d \end{vmatrix} = ad - bc.
</md>
</p>

<example xml:id="example-cross-compute">
<title>Computing a cross product</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the cross product <m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf v</m> if
<m>\mathbf u = \langle 1,-1,2\rangle</m> and
<m>\mathbf v = \langle 3,-2,1\rangle</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<md>
<mrow>
\mathbf u \times \mathbf v \amp=
\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf i \amp \mathbf j \amp \mathbf k \\
1 \amp -1 \amp 2 \\
3 \amp -2 \amp 1
\end{vmatrix}
=
\begin{vmatrix} -1 \amp 2 \\ -2 \amp 1 \end{vmatrix}\mathbf i
- \begin{vmatrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 3 \amp 1 \end{vmatrix}\mathbf j
+ \begin{vmatrix} 1 \amp -1 \\ 3 \amp -2 \end{vmatrix}\mathbf k
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= (-1+4)\mathbf i - (1-6)\mathbf j + (-2+3)\mathbf k
= 3\mathbf i + 5\mathbf j + \mathbf k
= \langle 3,5,1\rangle.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-perpendicular-vector">
<title>Computing a vector perpendicular to two other vectors</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the points <m>A(1,-1,2)</m>, <m>B(2,0,-1)</m>, and
<m>C(0,-2,3)</m>. Find a unit vector which is orthogonal to both
<m>\vec{AB}</m> and <m>\vec{AC}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We first compute the two vectors:
<md>
<mrow>\vec{AB} \amp= \langle 1,1,-3\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\vec{AC} \amp= \langle -1,-1,1\rangle.</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
The cross product <m>\vec{AB} \times \vec{AC}</m> is orthogonal to both
vectors <m>\vec{AB}</m> and <m>\vec{AC}</m>:
<md>
<mrow>
\vec{AB} \times \vec{AC} \amp=
\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf i \amp \mathbf j \amp \mathbf k \\
1 \amp 1 \amp -3 \\
-1 \amp -1 \amp 1
\end{vmatrix}
=
\begin{vmatrix} 1 \amp -3 \\ -1 \amp 1 \end{vmatrix}\mathbf i
- \begin{vmatrix} 1 \amp -3 \\ -1 \amp 1 \end{vmatrix}\mathbf j
+ \begin{vmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp -1 \end{vmatrix}\mathbf k
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= (1-3)\mathbf i - (1-3)\mathbf j + (-1+1)\mathbf k
= -2\mathbf i + 2\mathbf j
= \langle -2,2,0\rangle.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
The unit vector in question is then computed as follows:
<md xml:id="eq-perpendicular-unit">
       \begin{aligned}
\mathbf n \amp = \frac{\vec{AB} \times \vec{AC}}{\left|\vec{AB} \times \vec{AC}\right|}\\
\amp = \frac{1}{\sqrt 8}\langle -2,2,0\rangle
= \left\langle -\frac{1}{\sqrt 2},\frac{1}{\sqrt 2},0\right\rangle
= -\frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\mathbf i + \frac{1}{\sqrt 2}\mathbf j.
\end{aligned}
</md>
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-normal-vector-video">
<caption>
The unit vector <m>\mathbf n</m> is orthogonal to both
<m>\vec{AB}</m> and <m>\vec{AC}</m>, and hence to the plane through the
points <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-normal-vector"
       source="videos/normal_vector_3d_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-triangle-area">
<title>Computing the area of a triangle</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the area of the triangle with the vertices <m>A(1,-1,2)</m>,
<m>B(2,0,-1)</m>, and <m>C(0,-2,3)</m>. Note that these are the same
points that we had in the previous example.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
To find the area of the triangle, we first compute the area of the
parallelogram built on <m>\vec{AB}</m> and <m>\vec{AC}</m>, shown in
<xref ref="fig-triangle-area"/>, and then divide by <m>2</m>:
<md>
<mrow>
\text{Area of the parallelogram} \amp=
\left|\vec{AB} \times \vec{AC}\right| = \sqrt 8 = 2\sqrt 2
</mrow>
<mrow>
\text{Area of the triangle} \amp=
\frac12 \left|\vec{AB} \times \vec{AC}\right| = \sqrt 2.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-triangle-area">
<caption>
The triangle built on the vectors <m>\vec{AB}</m> and <m>\vec{AC}</m>
is half of the corresponding parallelogram.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-triangle-area">
<diagram dimensions="(340,200)" margins="10">
<definition>b=(4,0)</definition>
<definition>c=(1.5,3)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.5,-0.6,6,3.6)">
<line at="tri-side-bc" endpoints="(c, b)" stroke="black" dash="4 4"/>
<line at="tri-side-top" endpoints="(c, b+c)" stroke="red" dash="4 4"/>
<line at="tri-side-right" endpoints="(b, b+c)" stroke="blue" dash="4 4"/>
<vector at="tri-vec-ac" v="c" tail="(0,0)" stroke="blue"/>
<vector at="tri-vec-ab" v="b" tail="(0,0)" stroke="red"/>
<label at="tri-ac-label" anchor="(0.75,1.5)" alignment="nw" color="blue">
<m>\vec{AC}</m>
</label>
<label at="tri-ab-label" anchor="(2,0)" alignment="s" color="red">
<m>\vec{AB}</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations text="A triangle formed by the vectors AB and AC drawn tail to tail from the vertex A, together with a dashed segment from the tip of AC to the tip of AB. Two more dashed segments complete the parallelogram built on AB and AC, showing that the triangle is half of the parallelogram.">
<annotation ref="tri-vec-ab" text="Vector AB, along the horizontal direction."/>
<annotation ref="tri-vec-ac" text="Vector AC, drawn from the same tail as AB."/>
<annotation ref="tri-side-bc" text="The dashed third side of the triangle, from the tip of AC to the tip of AB."/>
<annotation ref="tri-side-top" text="A dashed side completing the parallelogram, parallel to AB."/>
<annotation ref="tri-side-right" text="A dashed side completing the parallelogram, parallel to AC."/>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</example>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-cross-identity">
<statement>
<p>
Show that
<m>(\mathbf u - \mathbf v) \times (\mathbf u + \mathbf v) = 2(\mathbf u \times \mathbf v)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Using the distributive properties of the cross product from
<xref ref="thm-cross-properties"/>,
<md>
(\mathbf u - \mathbf v) \times (\mathbf u + \mathbf v)
= \mathbf u \times \mathbf u + \mathbf u \times \mathbf v
- \mathbf v \times \mathbf u - \mathbf v \times \mathbf v.
</md>
Since a vector is parallel to itself,
<m>\mathbf u \times \mathbf u = \mathbf 0</m> and
<m>\mathbf v \times \mathbf v = \mathbf 0</m> by
<xref ref="fact-parallel-vectors"/>. Also
<m>\mathbf v \times \mathbf u = -(\mathbf u \times \mathbf v)</m> by
<xref ref="thm-cross-properties"/>, so
<md>
(\mathbf u - \mathbf v) \times (\mathbf u + \mathbf v)
= \mathbf 0 + \mathbf u \times \mathbf v - \bigl(-(\mathbf u \times \mathbf v)\bigr) - \mathbf 0
= 2(\mathbf u \times \mathbf v).
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</section>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<section xml:id="sec-true-false">
<title>True or False?</title>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-true-false-1">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="yes">
<p>
Let <m>\vec u</m> and <m>\vec v</m> be two vectors in
<m>\mathbb R^3</m>. Then, <m>\vec u \times \text{proj}_{\vec v}\vec u</m>
is perpendicular to <m>\vec u - \text{proj}_{\vec v}\vec u</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
Note that <m>\vec u \times \text{proj}_{\vec v}\vec u</m> is perpendicular to both
<m>\vec u</m> and <m>\text{proj}_{\vec v}\vec u</m>, which gives:
<md xml:id="eq-true-false-1">
       \begin{aligned}
\left(\vec u \times \text{proj}_{\vec v}\vec u\right) \cdot
\left(\vec u - \text{proj}_{\vec v}\vec u\right)
\amp =
\left(\vec u \times \text{proj}_{\vec v}\vec u\right) \cdot \vec u
- \left(\vec u \times \text{proj}_{\vec v}\vec u\right) \cdot \text{proj}_{\vec v}\vec u\\
= 0 - 0 = 0,
\end{aligned}
</md>
and therefore <m>\vec u \times \text{proj}_{\vec v}\vec u</m> is
perpendicular to <m>\vec u - \text{proj}_{\vec v}\vec u</m>.
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-true-false-2">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="yes">
<p>
Let <m>\vec u</m> and <m>\vec v</m> be two standard unit vectors in
<m>\mathbb R^3</m>. Then,
<m>\left(\vec u \cdot \vec v\right)^2 + \left|\vec v \times \vec u\right|^2 = 1</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
Note that:
<md>
<mrow>
\left(\vec u \cdot \vec v\right)^2 + \left|\vec v \times \vec u\right|^2
\amp= |\vec u|^2|\vec v|^2\cos^2\theta + |\vec u|^2|\vec v|^2\sin^2\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= |\vec u|^2|\vec v|^2\left(\cos^2\theta + \sin^2\theta\right)
= |\vec u|^2|\vec v|^2.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
Now, since <m>\vec u</m> and <m>\vec v</m> are standard unit vectors, we
have <m>|\vec u| = |\vec v| = 1</m>, which means:
<md xml:id="eq-true-false-3">
\left(\vec u \cdot \vec v\right)^2 + \left|\vec v \times \vec u\right|^2
= |\vec u|^2|\vec v|^2 = 1.
</md>
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

</section>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<section xml:id="sec-torque">
<title>Applications</title>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-torque">
<title>Torque</title>

<p>
One of the most important physical applications of the cross product is
<term>torque</term>, the rotational analog of force. A force makes an
object accelerate; a torque makes it <em>rotate</em>. How effective a
force is at producing rotation depends not only on its magnitude, but
also on <em>where</em> it is applied and in <em>what direction</em>.
Think of pushing a door: pushing hard near the hinge barely moves it,
while a light push at the doorknob swings it open easily. Pushing at the
doorknob but <em>toward</em> the hinge does nothing at all. The cross
product captures all three effects at once.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-torque">
<title>Torque</title>
<statement>
<p>
Suppose a force <m>\vec F</m> is applied at a point whose position
vector relative to the axis of rotation is <m>\vec r</m>. The
<term>torque</term> produced by <m>\vec F</m> is the vector
<md xml:id="eq-torque-def" number="yes">
\vec\tau = \vec r \times \vec F.
</md>
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
Everything we know about the cross product now translates into physics.
By <xref ref="eq-cross-geometric"/>, the magnitude of the torque is
<md xml:id="eq-torque-magnitude" number="yes">
|\vec\tau| = |\vec r||\vec F||\sin\theta|,
</md>

where <m>\theta</m> is the angle between <m>\vec r</m> and
<m>\vec F</m>, and the direction of <m>\vec\tau</m> is given by the
right-hand rule: <m>\vec\tau</m> points along the axis of rotation, and
curling the fingers of the right hand from <m>\vec r</m> toward
<m>\vec F</m> gives the sense of the rotation. Torque has units of
meter-newtons (<m>\text{m}\cdot\text{N}</m>) in SI.
</p>

<p>
The quantity in <xref ref="eq-torque-magnitude"/> can be grouped in two
useful ways:
<md>
<mrow>
|\vec\tau| \amp= \underbrace{\left(|\vec r|\sin\theta\right)}_{r_\perp}|\vec F|
 = r_\perp |\vec F|,
</mrow>
<mrow>
|\vec\tau| \amp= |\vec r|\underbrace{\left(|\vec F|\sin\theta\right)}_{F_\perp}
 = |\vec r|\, F_\perp.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
The first grouping says the torque is the force times the
<term>lever arm</term> <m>r_\perp</m>, the perpendicular distance from
the axis to the line along which the force acts. The second says it is
the distance <m>|\vec r|</m> times <m>F_\perp</m>, the component of the
force perpendicular to <m>\vec r</m>; the parallel component
<m>F_\parallel</m> points straight at the axis and produces no rotation.
</p>

<fact xml:id="fact-zero-torque">
<title>Zero Torque</title>
<statement>
<p>
By <xref ref="eq-parallel-vectors"/>, if <m>\vec F</m> is parallel to
<m>\vec r</m> then <m>\vec\tau = \vec r \times \vec F = \vec 0</m>: a
force directed straight at (or away from) the axis produces no rotation.
This is why you cannot open a door by pushing toward its hinge.
</p>
</statement>
</fact>

<example xml:id="example-biceps-torque">
<title>Biceps torque</title>
<statement>
<p>
The biceps muscle exerts a vertical force of
<m>|\vec F| = 700</m> N on the lower arm, attached at a point
<m>5.0</m> cm from the elbow joint (the axis of rotation). Calculate
the torque about the elbow when
</p>
<p>
<ol>
<li><p>the forearm is horizontal, and</p></li>
<li><p>the forearm makes a <m>30^\circ</m> angle below the horizontal.</p></li>
</ol>
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig-biceps-torque-video">
<caption>
The torque <m>\vec\tau = \vec r \times \vec F</m> of the biceps force
about the elbow. When the forearm drops <m>30^\circ</m> below the
horizontal, the angle between <m>\vec r</m> and <m>\vec F</m> becomes
<m>120^\circ</m>, the lever arm <m>r_\perp = |\vec r|\sin\theta</m>
shortens, and the torque decreases from <m>35</m> to <m>30</m>
<m>\text{m}\cdot\text{N}</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-biceps-torque"
       source="videos/biceps_torque_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
<figure xml:id="fig-biceps-torque">
  <caption>The biceps exerts a 700 N force on the forearm: (a) forearm horizontal, with the muscle attached 5.0 cm from the elbow axis; (b) forearm at 30&#xb0; below horizontal, with lever arm <m>R</m>.</caption>
  <image source="images/biceps-side-by-side.png" width="100%">
    <description>
<p>
Side-by-side diagrams of an arm. In (a), the forearm is horizontal and a 700 N biceps force acts 5.0 cm from the elbow axis. In (b), the forearm is angled 30 degrees below horizontal, making a 60 degree angle with the vertical, with lever arm R.
</p>
</description>
  </image>
</figure>
</statement>

<solution>

<p>
Place the elbow at the origin and let the force act in the plane
<m>z = 0</m>, with <m>\vec F = \langle 0, 700, 0\rangle</m> pointing
straight up.
</p>

<p>
<em>Part 1.</em> The forearm is horizontal, so
<m>\vec r = \langle 0.050, 0, 0\rangle</m> and the angle between
<m>\vec r</m> and <m>\vec F</m> is <m>\theta = 90^\circ</m>. Then
<md>
<mrow>
\vec\tau = \vec r \times \vec F \amp=
\begin{vmatrix}
\vec i \amp \vec j \amp \vec k \\
0.050 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 700 \amp 0
\end{vmatrix}
= \langle 0, 0, (0.050)(700)\rangle
= \langle 0, 0, 35 \rangle,
</mrow>
</md>
so <m>|\vec\tau| = 35\ \text{m}\cdot\text{N}</m>. Equivalently,
<m>|\vec\tau| = |\vec r||\vec F|\sin 90^\circ = (0.050)(700)(1) = 35\
\text{m}\cdot\text{N}</m>. The torque points in the direction
<m>+\vec k</m> (out of the page), which by the right-hand rule
corresponds to a counterclockwise rotation of the forearm about the
elbow.
</p>

<p>
<em>Part 2.</em> Now the forearm points <m>30^\circ</m> below the
horizontal, so
<md xml:id="eq-biceps-r-vector" number="yes">
\vec r = 0.050\,\langle \cos(-30^\circ), \sin(-30^\circ), 0\rangle
       = \langle 0.0433, -0.025, 0\rangle,
</md>

while <m>\vec F = \langle 0, 700, 0\rangle</m> is unchanged. The cross
product gives
<md>
<mrow>
\vec\tau = \vec r \times \vec F \amp=
\begin{vmatrix}
\vec i \amp \vec j \amp \vec k \\
0.0433 \amp -0.025 \amp 0 \\
0 \amp 700 \amp 0
\end{vmatrix}
= \langle 0, 0, (0.0433)(700) - (-0.025)(0)\rangle
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \langle 0, 0, 30.3 \rangle \approx \langle 0, 0, 30 \rangle.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
<p>
So <m>|\vec\tau| \approx 30\ \text{m}\cdot\text{N}</m>, again
counterclockwise. To check with <xref ref="eq-torque-magnitude"/>: the
vector <m>\vec r</m> sits at <m>-30^\circ</m> and <m>\vec F</m> at
<m>90^\circ</m>, so the angle between them is <m>\theta = 120^\circ</m>,
and
<md xml:id="eq-biceps-check" number="yes">
|\vec\tau| = |\vec r||\vec F|\sin 120^\circ
= (0.050)(700)\left(\tfrac{\sqrt 3}{2}\right)
\approx 30\ \text{m}\cdot\text{N}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Note that <m>\sin 120^\circ = \sin 60^\circ</m>, so this agrees with
the lever-arm point of view: the lever arm has shortened to
<m>r_\perp = (0.050)\sin 60^\circ \approx 0.043</m> m. The arm exerts
less torque at this angle than when the forearm is horizontal —
weight machines at gyms are often designed to account for exactly this
variation.
</p>

</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-circular-motion">
<title>Circular Motion from a Perpendicular Force</title>

<p>
Through <xref ref="act-cross-circular-motion"/>, we will show that <em>a force of constant
magnitude that always points perpendicular to the velocity drives the
particle around a circle.</em> We then apply it to a charged particle in a
magnetic field.
</p>

<p>
Both of the products you have met in this chapter appear, and they do
genuinely different jobs. Watch for them:
<ul>
<li>
<p>
the <term>dot product</term> is used, through <m>\vec F\cdot\vec v</m>, to determine whether
the speed of a particle acted upon by a perpendicular force can change,
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
the <term>cross product</term> is used to determine the
direction and magnitude of the force that keeps a charged
particle moving in a uniform magnetic field.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>

<activity xml:id="act-cross-circular-motion">
<title>Circular Motion from a Perpendicular Force</title>

<!-- ===================== PART I ===================== -->

<task xml:id="task-cm-speed">
<statement>
<p>
A particle of mass <m>m</m> moves under a force <m>\vec F</m> of constant
magnitude that is always perpendicular to its velocity <m>\vec v</m>.
Show that the particle's <em>speed</em> is constant.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Since <m>\vec F</m> is perpendicular to <m>\vec v</m>, we have
<md>
\vec F\cdot\vec v = 0\text{.}
</md>
</p>

<p>
Now consider the square of the speed, <m>v^2 = \vec v\cdot\vec v</m>. Using the
product rule for the dot product<fn>The product rule holds for the dot
product just as it does for ordinary multiplication:
<m>\dfrac{d}{dt}\left(\vec u\cdot\vec v\right)
= \dfrac{d\vec u}{dt}\cdot\vec v + \vec u\cdot\dfrac{d\vec v}{dt}</m>.
Taking <m>\vec u = \vec v</m> makes the two terms equal, which gives the
step used here,
<m>\dfrac{d}{dt}\left(\vec v\cdot\vec v\right)
= 2\,\vec v\cdot\dfrac{d\vec v}{dt}</m>.</fn> and then Newton's second law
<m>m\,\dfrac{d\vec v}{dt} = \vec F</m>,
<md>
<mrow>\frac{d}{dt}\left(v^2\right) \amp = \frac{d}{dt}
   \left(\vec v\cdot\vec v\right)
   = 2\,\vec v\cdot\frac{d\vec v}{dt}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{2}{m}\,\vec v\cdot\vec F = 0\text{.}</mrow>
</md>
So <m>v^2</m> never changes, and hence neither does the speed <m>v</m>.
</p>

<p>
<term>Why the dot product is the right tool here.</term> The quantity
<m>\vec F\cdot\vec v</m> is the rate at which the force does work on the
particle. A force with a forward component speeds the particle up; a force
with a backward component slows it down. Only the part of <m>\vec F</m>
lying along <m>\vec v</m> can change the speed, and the dot product is
exactly the mathematical tool that isolates that part. When it vanishes, can 
only change the direction of the velocity but not its magnitude, and so can 
only <em>turn</em> the particle, while the speed remains constant.
See <xref ref="fig-cm-dotproduct"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cm-dotproduct">
<caption>
Only the component of <m>\vec F</m> along <m>\vec v</m> can change the
speed. On the left that component is non-zero, so the particle speeds up;
on the right <m>\vec F\perp\vec v</m>, the component is zero, and the speed
cannot change.
</caption>
<image width="92%">
<description>
<p>
Two panels. In the left panel a force arrow leans forward over the
direction of motion and its component along the velocity is drawn in
green, so the dot product is non-zero and the speed changes. In the right
panel the force is perpendicular to the velocity, marked with a right
angle, so it has no component along the velocity, the dot product is zero,
and the speed stays constant.
</p>
</description>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cm-dotproduct">
      <diagram dimensions="(660,270)" margins="14">
      <definition>pathA(t) = (t, 0.09*t^2)</definition>
      <definition>pathB(t) = (t + 9, 0.09*t^2)</definition>
      <coordinates bbox="(-3.0,-2.4,13.6,4.4)">

      <!-- ============ PANEL A : force has a component along v ============ -->
      <parametric-curve at="da-path" function="pathA" domain="(-2.4,3.4)"
                        stroke="#BBBBBB" thickness="2"/>
      <vector at="da-v" tail="(0,0)" v="(3.2,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
      <vector at="da-F" tail="(0,0)" v="(1.6712,1.9919)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
      <line at="da-drop" p1="(1.6712,1.9919)" p2="(1.6712,0)"
            stroke="#888888" thickness="1.4" dash="4 3"/>
      <vector at="da-proj" tail="(0,0)" v="(1.6712,0)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="4"/>
      <point at="da-P" p="(0,0)" fill="black" size="3"/>
      <label font-size="18" at="da-vl" anchor="(3.3,0.02)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2"><m>\vec v</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="da-Fl" anchor="(1.74,2.06)" alignment="ne" color="#D55E00"><m>\vec F</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="da-projl" anchor="(0.84,-0.16)" alignment="s" color="#009E73">
      <m>\text{component along }\vec v</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="da-concl" anchor="(0.5,-1.35)" alignment="c">
      <m>\vec F\cdot\vec v \ne 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \text{speed changes}</m>
      </label>

      <!-- ============ PANEL B : force purely perpendicular ============ -->
      <parametric-curve at="db-path" function="pathB" domain="(-2.4,3.4)"
                        stroke="#BBBBBB" thickness="2"/>
      <vector at="db-v" tail="(9,0)" v="(3.2,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
      <vector at="db-F" tail="(9,0)" v="(0,2.6)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
      <angle-marker font-size="18" at="db-right" points="((10,0),(9,0),(9,1))" radius="20" stroke="black"/>
      <point at="db-P" p="(9,0)" fill="black" size="3"/>
      <label font-size="18" at="db-vl" anchor="(12.3,0.02)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2"><m>\vec v</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="db-Fl" anchor="(9,2.7)" alignment="n" color="#D55E00"><m>\vec F</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="db-zero" anchor="(9.55,-0.55)" alignment="e" color="#009E73">
      <m>\text{no component along }\vec v</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="db-concl" anchor="(9.5,-1.35)" alignment="c">
      <m>\vec F\cdot\vec v = 0 \;\Rightarrow\; \text{speed constant}</m>
      </label>

      </coordinates>

      <annotations>
      <annotation ref="figure" text="Two panels comparing a force that is not perpendicular to the velocity with one that is. In the left panel the force arrow leans forward and has a green component along the velocity, so the dot product is nonzero and the speed changes. In the right panel the force is straight up, perpendicular to the velocity, so it has no component along the velocity, the dot product is zero, and the speed stays constant.">
        <annotation ref="da-F" text="A force leaning forward, at an acute angle to the velocity."/>
        <annotation ref="da-proj" text="The component of that force along the velocity, drawn in green; it is nonzero, so the speed changes."/>
        <annotation ref="db-F" text="A force perpendicular to the velocity."/>
        <annotation ref="db-right" text="A right angle marker showing the force is perpendicular to the velocity, so its component along the velocity is zero."/>
      </annotation>
      </annotations>
      </diagram>
      </prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cm-turn">
<statement>
<p>
During a short time interval <m>\Delta t</m>, show that the
<em>direction</em> of the velocity turns through the angle
<md>
\Delta\theta = \frac{F}{mv}\,\Delta t\text{.}
</md>
(Hint: draw the two velocity vectors, which have equal length, from a
common tail.)
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
By the previous task the velocity never changes length; it only swings
around. So <m>\vec v(t)</m> and <m>\vec v(t+\Delta t)</m> are two vectors of
the <em>same</em> length <m>v</m>, separated by the small angle
<m>\Delta\theta</m> we are after. Drawn from a common tail they form an
isosceles triangle whose third side is
<m>\Delta\vec v = \vec v(t+\Delta t) - \vec v(t)</m>, as in
<xref ref="fig-cm-deltav"/>.
</p>

<p>
The tips of the two vectors both lie on a circle of radius <m>v</m>. The
straight side <m>\Delta\vec v</m> is the chord cutting off the arc of angle
<m>\Delta\theta</m> on that circle, and for a small angle a chord is very
nearly the arc it subtends:
<md>
\left|\Delta\vec v\right| = 2v\sin\frac{\Delta\theta}{2}
  \approx v\,\Delta\theta\text{.}
</md>
</p>

<p>
On the other hand Newton's second law over the short interval gives
<md>
\left|\Delta\vec v\right| \approx \frac{F}{m}\,\Delta t\text{.}
</md>
Equating the two expressions for <m>\left|\Delta\vec v\right|</m>,
<md>
v\,\Delta\theta = \frac{F}{m}\,\Delta t
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
\Delta\theta = \frac{F}{mv}\,\Delta t\text{,}
</md>
which is what we wanted. Notice the rate of turning
<m>\Delta\theta/\Delta t = F/(mv)</m> is a <em>constant</em>, since
<m>F</m>, <m>m</m> and <m>v</m> all are: the particle swings around at a
steady angular rate.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cm-deltav">
<caption>
The two velocities have equal length <m>v</m> (dashed arc), so the triangle
is isosceles and the chord <m>\Delta\vec v</m> has length
<m>\approx v\,\Delta\theta</m>.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<description>
<p>
Two velocity arrows of equal length v are drawn from a common tail,
separated by a small angle delta theta, with a dashed circular arc of
radius v joining their tips to show the lengths are equal. A third arrow,
delta v, runs from the first tip to the second; its length is about v
times delta theta.
</p>
</description>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cm-deltav">
      <diagram dimensions="(470,336)" margins="14">
      <coordinates bbox="(-1.4,-2.1,7.0,3.9)">

      <!-- dashed arc of radius v, showing both velocities have the SAME length -->
      <arc at="dv-arc" center="(0,0)" radius="4" range="(0,40)" degrees="yes"
           stroke="#999999" thickness="1.4" dash="4 3"/>

      <!-- the two velocity vectors, equal in length -->
      <vector at="dv-v1" tail="(0,0)" v="(4,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
      <vector at="dv-v2" tail="(0,0)" v="(3.0642,2.5712)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>

      <!-- their difference, which is what the force produces -->
      <vector at="dv-dv" tail="(4,0)" v="(-0.9358,2.5712)" stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>

      <angle-marker font-size="18" at="dv-ang" points="((1,0),(0,0),(0.766,0.643))" radius="46"
                    stroke="black"><m>\Delta\theta</m></angle-marker>

      <point at="dv-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" size="3"/>

      <label font-size="18" at="dv-l1" anchor="(4.08,-0.06)" alignment="se" color="#0072B2">
      <m>\vec v(t)</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="dv-l2" anchor="(3.02,2.66)" alignment="ne" color="#0072B2">
      <m>\vec v(t+\Delta t)</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="dv-ldv" anchor="(3.62,1.30)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
      <m>\Delta\vec v</m>
      </label>

      <!-- the two lengths, marked on the vectors themselves -->
      <label font-size="18" at="dv-len1" anchor="(2.0,-0.14)" alignment="s" color="#666666"><m>v</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="dv-len2" anchor="(1.42,1.35)" alignment="nw" color="#666666"><m>v</m></label>

      <label font-size="18" at="dv-note1" anchor="(-1.2,-1.15)" alignment="e">
      <m>\text{equal lengths} \;\Rightarrow\; |\Delta\vec v| \approx v\,\Delta\theta</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="dv-note2" anchor="(-1.2,-1.80)" alignment="e">
      <m>\text{Newton:}\; |\Delta\vec v| = \tfrac{F}{m}\Delta t
        \;\Rightarrow\; \Delta\theta = \tfrac{F}{mv}\Delta t</m>
      </label>

      </coordinates>

      <annotations>
      <annotation ref="figure" text="Two velocity vectors of equal length v are drawn from a common tail, separated by a small angle delta theta. A dashed circular arc of radius v joins their tips, confirming the lengths are equal. A third arrow, delta v, runs from the tip of the first to the tip of the second. Because the triangle is isosceles and the angle is small, the length of delta v is approximately v times delta theta.">
        <annotation ref="dv-arc" text="A dashed arc of radius v joining the two arrow tips, showing both velocities have the same length."/>
        <annotation ref="dv-v1" text="The velocity at time t."/>
        <annotation ref="dv-v2" text="The velocity a short time delta t later, the same length but turned through delta theta."/>
        <annotation ref="dv-dv" text="The change in velocity, delta v, joining the two tips; its length is approximately v times delta theta."/>
        <annotation ref="dv-ang" text="The small angle delta theta through which the velocity has turned."/>
      </annotation>
      </annotations>
      </diagram>
      </prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cm-radius">
<statement>
<p>
Combine <m>\Delta s = v\,\Delta t</m> with the arc-length formula
<m>\Delta s = R\,\Delta\theta</m> to prove that
<md>
R = \frac{mv^2}{F}\text{.}
</md>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
In time <m>\Delta t</m> the particle covers the arc length
<m>\Delta s = v\,\Delta t</m>. If that arc bends through the angle
<m>\Delta\theta</m>, then the radius <m>R</m> of the arc is defined by
<m>\Delta s = R\,\Delta\theta</m>. Solving for <m>R</m> and substituting
both expressions,
<md>
R = \frac{\Delta s}{\Delta\theta}
  = \frac{v\,\Delta t}{\dfrac{F}{mv}\,\Delta t}
  = \frac{mv^2}{F}\text{.}
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cm-circle">
<statement>
<p>
Conclude that the trajectory is a circle.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The quantity <m>R = mv^2/F</m> is built from <m>m</m>, <m>v</m> and
<m>F</m>, every one of which is constant — <m>v</m> because of the first
task, and <m>F</m> by hypothesis. So the path bends through equal angles
over equal arc lengths, everywhere along the trajectory: its radius of
curvature is the same constant <m>R</m> at every point.
</p>

<p>
A plane curve with constant radius of curvature is a circle. Concretely,
the centre of the turn always lies a fixed distance <m>R</m> from the
particle in the direction of <m>\vec F</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cm-circle">
<caption>
The velocity is tangent to the circle and the force points straight at the
centre. Equal arcs <m>\Delta s</m> subtend equal angles
<m>\Delta\theta</m>, and the ratio of the two is the constant radius
<m>R = mv^2/F</m>.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<description>
<p>
A circle of radius R centred at O. At a point on the circle the velocity
arrow is tangent and the force arrow points from the particle straight in
toward the centre, meeting the velocity at a right angle. Two dashed radii
mark a small angle delta theta at the centre, and the arc between them,
of length delta s equals R delta theta, is highlighted.
</p>
</description>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cm-circle">
      <diagram dimensions="(430,459)" margins="14">
      <coordinates bbox="(-4.3,-4.9,4.5,4.5)">

      <!-- the circular trajectory -->
      <circle at="cc-circle" center="(0,0)" radius="3"
              stroke="#9EC5E8" thickness="2" fill="none"/>

      <!-- the two radii bounding the small turn -->
      <line at="cc-r1" p1="(0,0)" p2="(1.7207,2.4575)" stroke="#999999" thickness="1.4" dash="4 3"/>
      <line at="cc-r2" p1="(0,0)" p2="(0.2615,2.9886)" stroke="#999999" thickness="1.4" dash="4 3"/>
      <angle-marker font-size="18" at="cc-dtheta" points="((1.7207,2.4575),(0,0),(0.2615,2.9886))"
                    radius="38" stroke="black"><m>\Delta\theta</m></angle-marker>

      <!-- the arc actually travelled -->
      <arc at="cc-ds" center="(0,0)" radius="3" range="(55,85)" degrees="yes"
           stroke="#009E73" thickness="5"/>

      <!-- velocity (tangent) and force (toward the centre) at the particle -->
      <vector at="cc-v" tail="(1.7207,2.4575)" v="(-1.4745,1.0324)"
              stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
      <vector at="cc-F" tail="(1.7207,2.4575)" v="(-0.8604,-1.2288)"
              stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
      <angle-marker font-size="18" at="cc-right"
                    points="((0.9015,3.0311),(1.7207,2.4575),(1.1471,1.6383))"
                    radius="18" stroke="black"/>

      <point at="cc-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black" size="3"/>
      <point at="cc-P" p="(1.7207,2.4575)" fill="black" size="3.5"/>

      <label font-size="18" at="cc-Ol" anchor="(-0.12,-0.12)" alignment="sw"><m>O</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="cc-Rl" anchor="(0.10,1.62)" alignment="w" color="#666666"><m>R</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="cc-vl" anchor="(0.20,3.56)" alignment="nw" color="#0072B2"><m>\vec v</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="cc-Fl" anchor="(1.40,1.92)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00"><m>\vec F</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="cc-dsl" anchor="(1.18,3.20)" alignment="ne" color="#009E73">
      <m>\Delta s = R\,\Delta\theta</m>
      </label>

      <label font-size="18" at="cc-note1" anchor="(-4.1,-3.55)" alignment="e">
      <m>\Delta s = v\,\Delta t \quad\text{and}\quad \Delta\theta = \tfrac{F}{mv}\Delta t</m>
      </label>
      <label font-size="18" at="cc-note2" anchor="(-4.1,-4.35)" alignment="e">
      <m>\Rightarrow\;\; R = \dfrac{\Delta s}{\Delta\theta} = \dfrac{mv^2}{F}
        \;\;\text{— a constant}</m>
      </label>

      </coordinates>

      <annotations>
      <annotation ref="figure" text="A circular trajectory with centre O and radius R. At a point on the circle the velocity arrow is drawn tangent to the circle and the force arrow points from the particle straight in toward the centre, the two meeting at a right angle. Two dashed radii mark off a small angle delta theta at the centre, and the arc of the circle between them, of length delta s equals R times delta theta, is highlighted. Combining delta s equals v delta t with delta theta equals F over m v times delta t gives R equals m v squared over F, a constant.">
        <annotation ref="cc-circle" text="The circular path of the particle."/>
        <annotation ref="cc-v" text="The velocity, tangent to the circle."/>
        <annotation ref="cc-F" text="The force, pointing from the particle straight in toward the centre, perpendicular to the velocity."/>
        <annotation ref="cc-right" text="A right angle marker between the velocity and the force."/>
        <annotation ref="cc-ds" text="The arc of length delta s travelled during the short time interval."/>
        <annotation ref="cc-dtheta" text="The angle delta theta subtended at the centre by that arc."/>
      </annotation>
      </annotations>
      </diagram>
      </prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</task>

<!-- ===================== PART II ===================== -->

<task xml:id="task-cm-magnitude">
<statement>
<p>
A particle of charge <m>q</m> enters a uniform magnetic field
<m>\vec B</m> with its velocity <em>perpendicular</em> to the field. The
magnetic force is
<md>
\vec F_B = q\,\vec v\times\vec B\text{.}
</md>
Show that this force has magnitude <m>|q|\,vB</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The length of a cross product is
<m>\left|\vec v\times\vec B\right| = vB\sin\alpha</m>, where <m>\alpha</m>
is the angle between <m>\vec v</m> and <m>\vec B</m>. Here the velocity is
perpendicular to the field, so <m>\alpha = 90^\circ</m> and
<m>\sin\alpha = 1</m>. Therefore
<md>
\left|\vec F_B\right| = |q|\left|\vec v\times\vec B\right|
 = |q|\,vB\sin 90^\circ = |q|\,vB\text{.}
</md>
Since <m>v</m> is constant (we are about to confirm this) and <m>B</m> is
uniform, this magnitude is constant — the first hypothesis of part
<xref ref="task-cm-speed" text="local"/>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cm-perp">
<statement>
<p>
Explain why this force is <em>always</em> perpendicular to the velocity —
not just at the initial instant, but at every moment of the motion.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
This is the defining property of the cross product: <m>\vec v\times\vec B</m>
is perpendicular to <em>both</em> factors, whatever they happen to be. So
<m>\vec F_B = q\,\vec v\times\vec B</m> is perpendicular to <m>\vec v</m>
automatically, at every instant, with no computation and no special
assumption about where the particle is or which way it is heading.
</p>

<p>
If you would like it verified rather than quoted, the dot product does it in
one line.<fn>Two facts are doing the work. First, the scalar triple product
<m>\left(\vec a\times\vec b\right)\cdot\vec c</m> equals the determinant
whose rows are <m>\vec a</m>, <m>\vec b</m> and <m>\vec c</m>. Second, a
determinant with two equal rows is zero, since interchanging those two rows
both leaves the determinant unchanged and reverses its sign.</fn> Taking
<m>\vec c = \vec v</m>,
<md>
\vec F_B\cdot\vec v
 = q\left(\vec v\times\vec B\right)\cdot\vec v
 = q\begin{vmatrix}
 v_1 \amp v_2 \amp v_3 \\
 B_1 \amp B_2 \amp B_3 \\
 v_1 \amp v_2 \amp v_3
 \end{vmatrix} = 0\text{.}
</md>
</p>

<p>
So such a
magnetic field can <em>never</em> change the speed of a charged particle,
no matter how strong it is. Here the magnetic force does no work and it solely 
changes the direction of the velocity.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="task-cm-orbit">
<statement>
<p>
Deduce that the particle moves in a circle, and find the radius of that
circle.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The previous two tasks establish exactly the two hypotheses of part
<xref ref="task-cm-speed" text="local"/>: the magnetic force has constant magnitude
<m>|q|vB</m>, and it is always perpendicular to the velocity. The argument
of parts <xref ref="task-cm-speed" text="local"/>–<xref ref="task-cm-circle" text="local"/> therefore
applies verbatim, and the particle travels in a circle at constant speed.
</p>

<p>
For the radius, put <m>F = |q|vB</m> into <m>R = mv^2/F</m>:
<md>
r = \frac{mv^2}{|q|vB} = \frac{mv}{|q|B}\text{.}
</md>
Equivalently, the magnetic force supplies precisely the inward force
<m>mv^2/r</m> that circular motion demands:
<md>
|q|vB = \frac{mv^2}{r}
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
r = \frac{mv}{|q|B}\text{.}
</md>
See <xref ref="fig-cm-magnetic"/>. Faster particles and heavier particles
travel on wider circles; stronger fields and larger charges wind them
tighter.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cm-magnetic">
<caption>
A positive charge in a uniform field <m>\vec B</m> directed into the page.
The force <m>q\,\vec v\times\vec B</m> is perpendicular to <m>\vec v</m>
automatically, so it never changes the speed — it only bends the path into
a circle of radius <m>r = mv/(|q|B)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<description>
<p>
A uniform magnetic field into the page, shown as a grid of small crosses.
A positive charge travels counter-clockwise around a circle of radius r
centred at O. At the rightmost point of the circle its velocity points
straight up, tangent to the circle, and the magnetic force q v cross B
points straight in toward the centre, at a right angle to the velocity.
</p>
</description>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-cm-magnetic">
      <diagram dimensions="(460,489)" margins="14">
      <coordinates bbox="(-4.6,-5.6,4.8,4.4)">

      <!-- uniform magnetic field INTO the page -->
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b00" anchor="(-3.7,-3.4)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b10" anchor="(-1.85,-3.4)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>
      <label font-size="18" at="mb-b20" anchor="(0,-3.4)" alignment="c" color="#7A5AA8"><m>\times</m></label>
